<header><p class="enc-kicker">State</p><h1 class="enc-title">Tanganyika Territory</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1916">1916</a>–<a href="/years/1961">1961</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as British Tanganyika</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Tanganyika Territory was the British-ruled part of former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-east-africa">German East Africa</a>, held as a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/league-of-nations-mandate">League of Nations mandate</a> from 1922 and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-nations">United Nations</a> trust territory from 1946. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> governed it through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indirect-rule">indirect rule</a>, keeping local kings and chiefs as native authorities. It became independent in 1961 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-nyerere">Julius Nyerere</a>, abolished the chieftaincies in 1963 and joined Zanzibar to form Tanzania in 1964.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/tanganyika-territory.jpg" alt="Tanganyika Territory" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Mysid · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Tanganyika_(1923%E2%80%931961).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> the area formed part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-east-africa">German East Africa</a>, which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> had declared a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> in 1885 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carl-peters">Carl Peters</a>. The Germans repressed the Maji Maji Rebellion of 1905 brutally and developed the colony around cash crops such as sisal, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coffee-economy">coffee</a> and cocoa; in 1913 sisal made up 30% of exports, and the Central Line railway linked much of the country to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dar-es-salaam">Dar es Salaam</a>.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a> and Belgian Congo forces gradually occupied it during the East Africa Campaign, although German resistance continued until 1918. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-versailles">Treaty of Versailles</a> the territory went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a>, apart from Ruanda-Urundi, assigned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kionga-triangle">Kionga Triangle</a>, assigned to Portuguese Mozambique. The British needed a new name and took it from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-tanganyika">Lake Tanganyika</a>, rejecting among others Smutsland, Windsorland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-kilimanjaro">Kilimanjaro</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The mandate was formalised on 20 July 1922, and on 22 March 1921 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belgium">Belgium</a> had ceded the Kigoma District to the territory. The British wanted a 'Black man's country' like <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nigeria">Nigeria</a> and adopted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indirect-rule">indirect rule</a>. Under the governor Sir Horace Bryatt, whose anti-German policy expelled Germans and halved the European population, ex-German plantations were sold to European companies. The Asian population rose from 8,698 in 1912 to 25,144 in 1931.</p><p>In 1927 Tanganyika joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/customs-union">customs union</a> of Kenya and Uganda, and cooperation grew through the East African High Commission, which lasted from 1948 to 1961. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tanganyika-african-national-union">Tanganyika African National Union</a>, founded in 1954 under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/julius-nyerere">Julius Nyerere</a>, won every seat in the 1958 election, and internal self-government followed in 1960. A party of British settlers, the United Tanganyikan Party, founded in 1956, was ineffective.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Tanganyika became independent on 9 December 1961, after Nyerere had met a British government representative to arrange the steps. He had become prime minister in 1960 under British rule and became president when it was declared a republic in 1962. Independence came peacefully, but the country was short of money and infrastructure, and refugees from the Congo, Burundi and Rwanda arrived. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zanzibar-revolution">Zanzibar Revolution</a> of 1964, Tanganyika merged with Zanzibar into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-republic-of-tanganyika-and-zanzibar">United Republic of Tanganyika and Zanzibar</a>, which became the United Republic of Tanzania on 26 April 1964.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Tanganyika Territory” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Bugabo became a native authority under German East Africa and later <b>British Tanganyika Territory</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-bugabo">Kingdom of Bugabo</a></li><li><blockquote>Ihangiro came under German East Africa in the 1890s and continued as a native authority under <b>British Tanganyika Territory</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-ihangiro">Kingdom of Ihangiro</a></li><li><blockquote>Kianja remained a native authority under German East Africa and <b>British Tanganyika Territory</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-kianja">Kingdom of Kianja</a></li><li><blockquote>Kiziba became a subordinate chiefdom under German East Africa and then <b>British Tanganyika Territory</b>, ruled through its kings as a native authority.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-kiziba">Kingdom of Kiziba</a></li><li><blockquote>The remaining kingdom passed under German East Africa and then <b>British Tanganyika Territory</b> as a native authority.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-kyamutwara">Kingdom of Kyamutwara</a></li></ul></section>
